# @NAME@

Scaffolded by `./new_game.sh @NAME@`.

```bash
./build_game.sh @NAME@          # debug; also release, asan, tsan
./run.sh                        # the build directory remembers which game it holds
```

`run.sh` takes no game argument because it does not need one — `build_game.sh` recorded
the choice in the CMake cache, which is also why `scripts/golden.sh` and
`scripts/baseline.py` need none either. Naming a different game rebuilds for it.

## What is here

| File | What it is |
|---|---|
| `@CLASS@.h` / `@CLASS@.cpp` | The `Game` subclass and its `SUBSTRATE_GAME` line |
| `CMakeLists.txt` | One `substrate_game(...)` call |
| `assets/` | Yours. `res:/name` searches here first, then the engine's tree |
| `shaders/` | Yours. A file named after an engine shader overrides it, for this game only |

Neither `assets/` nor `shaders/` exists until you make one, and both are optional.

## The rule worth knowing

**Nothing under `engine/` is edited to build a game.** The engine builds, tests and
sanitizes with `game/` absent from the tree entirely, which is what makes a dependency
leaking across that line a link error rather than something to catch in review. If this
game needs an engine change to do something ordinary, that is a gap in the public surface
worth reporting rather than patching around.

